PR #700  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Wrong ordering of Smip stack-pointer-swap relative to GPR save/restore causes interrupted context register state to reside on the handler stack, exposing it across privilege and thread boundaries
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:160-167  handle_exception
      The sp-swap (REG_L sp, TASK_TI_KERNEL_SP) at line 162 executes BEFORE save_from_x6_to_x31 at line 167. This means x10-x15 (a0-a5) are saved via sp-relative stores onto the kernel handler stack, not the interrupted context's own stack. Under Smip, cspspush must execute BEFORE the sp-swap so x10-x15 land on the interrupted-context-owned memory. The implicit invariant is 'no Smip hardware present' — if Smip cspspush were inserted naively after the sp-swap (or the swap were left as-is), interrupted-context x10-x15 state would leak onto a shared kernel stack.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:304-316  ret_from_exception
      The restore_from_x6_to_x31 at line 308 restores x10-x15 from the kernel handler stack (sp-relative PT_A0..PT_A5 loads). Under Smip, cspspop must restore x10-x15 from the interrupted context's own stack. If cspspop were inserted after restore_from_x6_to_x31 but before sret, the kernel-stack-loaded values would be overwritten — benign but wasteful. If cspspop were omitted, x10-x15 would be restored from the handler stack instead of the context-owned stack, violating the self-containment invariant. The implicit invariant is that the kernel-stack pt_regs holds the authoritative x10-x15 state, which breaks under Smip hardware save/restore.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/asm.h:128-155  save_from_x6_to_x31
      This macro unconditionally saves x10-x15 (PT_A0-PT_A5) via sp-relative stores (lines 133-138). Under Smip, x10-x15 for CLIC interrupt traps would be handled by cspspush hardware onto the old-context stack, and this macro's stores for those registers would be incorrect (saving to handler stack instead, or double-saving if cspspush already executed). The macro does not distinguish between interrupt-trap callers (where Smip applies) and exception/syscall callers (where software save may still be needed). The implicit invariant is that all callers want uniform software save; Smip breaks this by requiring a split path.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/asm.h:158-185  restore_from_x6_to_x31
      Mirror of save_from_x6_to_x31: unconditionally restores x10-x15 from sp-relative kernel-stack slots (lines 163-168). Under Smip, cspspop restores x10-x15 from the interrupted context's own stack, bypassing these loads. If this macro continues to execute for CLIC traps, it loads stale/incorrect values from the kernel stack that are then either used (info_leak/state corruption) or overwritten by cspspop (wasteful but harmless). The implicit invariant is that the kernel stack holds the true x10-x15 state.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:438-447  handle_riscv_irq
      handle_riscv_irq is a thin dispatcher that passes pt_regs to handle_arch_irq. It does not directly access regs->a0..a5. The pt_regs pointer it receives was constructed by handle_exception, which placed the frame on the kernel stack. Under Smip, the pt_regs->a0..a5 slots on the kernel stack would be stale (if cspspush saved x10-x15 to the interrupted context's stack instead), but RISC-V IRQ handlers use their own calling convention and do not typically read the interrupted context's a0-a5 from pt_regs. The security concern (info_leak from wrong stack) is not triggered by this function itself.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:334-338  handle_kernel_stack_overflow
      The overflow handler switches to the per-CPU overflow stack (lines 322-330) then calls save_from_x6_to_x31 (line 338) to save x10-x15 onto that overflow stack. Under Smip, cspspush would need to execute BEFORE the stack switch, but the original kernel stack has overflowed — a cspspush onto it would itself fault. This is an inherently ambiguous edge case for Smip: the 'interrupted context's own stack' is corrupted. Currently safe because handle_bad_stack kills the task, so register preservation semantics are moot. Risk: if Smip hardware blindly issues cspspush on a stack-overflow trap, it double-faults.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:381-408  call_on_irq_stack
      The IRQ stack switch at lines 393-396 happens AFTER pt_regs is already fully constructed on the original kernel stack (by handle_exception). The per-CPU IRQ stack is used only as the C execution stack for the handler; it does not hold interrupted-context register state. The sp here is the handler's working sp, not the sp that cspspush/cspspop reference. Smip semantics around context-owned-stack self-containment are not implicated — this is a handler-internal stack switch that is orthogonal to hardware register save/restore ordering.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/thread_info.h:53-79  thread_info
      The dual-stack-pointer structure (kernel_sp at line 61, user_sp at line 62) is the software equivalent of Smip's hardware sp-swap. Under Smip with CLIC interrupt nesting, the simple two-pointer model may be insufficient: cspspush/cspspop manage a hardware stack of saved context SPs, and software may need a nesting-depth counter or a saved-SP array to correctly unwind nested interrupts. The implicit invariant is that at most one kernel/user transition is in flight; CLIC nesting breaks this. Adding a nesting-depth or cspspush-state field here would require a corresponding asm-offsets entry.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/asm-offsets.c:40-43  asm_offsets
      This is a mechanical offset definition consumed by entry.S. It correctly generates TASK_TI_KERNEL_SP and TASK_TI_USER_SP for the current thread_info layout. If Smip adds a new field to thread_info (e.g., a nesting counter or cspspush state), a new OFFSET line must be added here, but this is a trivial follow-on change with no independent security implication. The current offsets are correct for the current non-Smip code.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:203-207  .Lsetup_trap_vector
      A single CSR write setting CSR_TVEC to handle_exception. This is correct for the current non-CLIC trap model. Under CLIC Smip, the trap vector mechanism changes fundamentally (vectored mode, CLIC-specific CSRs, separate entries per interrupt level), but this is a configuration change implied by the higher-priority entry.S rework. The one-line CSR write has no independent ordering or stack-ownership concern.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:102-137  SAVE_GUEST_GPRS
      Saves all guest GPRs including x10-x15 (a0-a5) into the kvm_vcpu_arch structure (host-owned VMM memory), not the guest's own stack. Under Smip, if guest Smip is virtualized, cspspush would need to save guest x10-x15 onto the GUEST's own stack, not the host's vcpu_arch. The current monolithic save would either double-save (if cspspush also fires) or save to the wrong memory domain. Currently safe because guest Smip virtualization is not implemented, but this is a secondary concern behind native kernel Smip support.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:65-100  RESTORE_GUEST_GPRS
      Mirror of SAVE_GUEST_GPRS: restores guest x10-x15 from kvm_vcpu_arch (host-owned memory). Under virtualized guest Smip, cspspop would restore guest x10-x15 from the guest's own stack, making these loads either redundant (if cspspop fires afterward and overwrites) or wrong (if the vcpu_arch slots are stale). Same scope caveat: guest Smip virtualization is a deferred concern behind native kernel Smip support.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:198-219  __kvm_riscv_switch_to
      SAVE_HOST_GPRS at line 199 saves host x10-x15 (as a1-a5) into the kvm_vcpu_arch structure (offset via a0), NOT onto the host's own kernel stack. This technically violates the Smip invariant that 'each execution context's saved state must be self-contained on its own memory' — the host's x10-x15 land in a shared VMM structure rather than host-owned stack memory. In practice, kvm_vcpu_arch is per-VCPU and trusted by the host, so the practical info_leak risk is low. But if a strict Smip model is enforced (hardware cspspush onto own stack only), this world-switch path is architecturally incompatible.

