PR #700  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Wrong ordering of Smip stack-pointer-swap relative to GPR save/restore causes interrupted context register state to reside on the handler stack, exposing it across privilege and thread boundaries
  [BUG] firmware/fw_base.S:672-690  _trap_handler
      TRAP_SAVE_AND_SETUP_SP_T0 (line 674) swaps SP to the handler-owned exception stack BEFORE TRAP_SAVE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_SP_T0 (line 678) saves a0-a5 (x10-x15). In Smip, cspspush must save x10-x15 to the interrupted context's own stack before any SP swap. The current ordering causes x10-x15 to land on the handler's shared stack, violating the self-contained-save invariant and enabling info_leak across contexts.
  [BUG] firmware/fw_base.S:694-724  _trap_handler_hyp
      Identical ordering flaw as _trap_handler: TRAP_SAVE_AND_SETUP_SP_T0 (line 696) swaps SP to handler stack before TRAP_SAVE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_SP_T0 (line 704) saves x10-x15. Same Smip violation with additional mstatusH/mtval2/mtinst handling. Must be fixed in tandem with _trap_handler for H-extension platforms.
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:489-529  TRAP_SAVE_AND_SETUP_SP_T0
